result.summary:
Read-only regulatory review of MYEZ at main 10135306 (legal docs, processors.json, the data inventory, the UG12/UF14 rulings and the ASC packet), plus GET-only checks of the live pages. getmyez.com/privacy.html, terms.html, support and ar/privacy.html all return 200. They are served by Railway directly ("Server: railway-hikari", no Cloudflare headers) and are dated October 11, 2026. The backend legal endpoints also return 200. This is engineering due diligence, not legal advice.

DIRECT ANSWERS
(1) Adding Ahmed Jameel in App Store Connect (Users and Access, +) is NOT a step before screenshots or submission. It is optional, and only the Account Holder (Hussain) or an Admin can do it. Choose the role for what he will do: Developer covers builds and TestFlight. Editing App Store details, App Privacy and screenshots, and submitting for review, need App Manager (or Admin). Apple's role matrix could not be read cell by cell (checkmarks are not in the page text), so treat the role split as UNVERIFIED. What still gates submission: Privacy Policy URL and Support URL set; two demo accounts plus the review notes; screenshots (TestFlight internal group, iPhone with Dynamic Island set); copyright (L1); DSA trader status; Content Rights; then Add for Review.

(2) How far MYEZ can go with storing and selling data. Storage: the current scope is normal and smaller than big-tech scope (no ad IDs, location, contacts or tracking). Each item is defensible as long as it is disclosed, kept no longer than needed (two "no fixed period" items need a period) and protected (backups, key rotation, private repo).
Selling: OpenAI, Google, Microsoft, Amazon and Meta all say they do not sell personal data. They do share aggregated or de-identified data (OpenAI also promises not to re-identify; Google gives advertisers aggregated reports and publishes Trends). UG12 (aggregated or anonymised statistics only, never personal data) is the same model.
What would make MYEZ a data broker or create a consent problem:
- selling row-level, event-level or pseudonymous records (including "de-identified" free-text queries);
- small demographic cells (governorate x age x gender in Bahrain; the repo's own cohort priors have n=5 cells);
- health or supplement queries cut by demographics;
- buyers that link the data or use it for ad targeting;
- any per-user transfer. That counts as tracking under Apple's definition and needs ATT and Tracking = Yes. Under the GCC laws it needs separate consent. It also conflicts with DPLA 3.3.3(B) (data used only for a function "directly relevant to the use of the Application") and with guideline 5.1.2(i).
The minimum to keep UG12 defensible, all text plus process: definitions; k >= 50 per cell with suppression; no free text, identifiers or health-by-demographic cuts; a no-re-identification, no-linking, no-onward-sale buyer contract (Apple's own DPLA wording for de-identified research data); an opt-out; a purpose stated in privacy s3.6; a documented legitimate-interest assessment; and counsel on DPLA 3.3.3(B) and 5.1.2(i) before the first actual sale.

(3) The free tier with limits is already in ToS s4 and matches usage_service.py TIER_LIMITS (3 lifetime, 3/day, 10/month). Two small improvements: a sentence that limits can differ per account and the free service can change; and adding "free with a daily and monthly allowance" to the ASC description, which is locked after release.

TOP ITEMS BEFORE PRESSING SUBMIT
- R01: the "we do not sell" sentence vs OpenAI's complimentary tokens. Reword so it is candid.
- R03: the GitHub repo is recorded as public, and rotation of the leaked keys and ADMIN_API_KEY is recorded as owed. Verify both and fix.
- R05: the UG12 definitions and no-re-identification text.
- R06: accept the processor DPAs (the policy already claims contracts with every processor).
- R19: the description line, because ASC locks it after release.

AFTER LAUNCH, WITH COUNSEL
- the bundled OpenAI training consent: give it its own opt-out with a second OpenAI project that has sharing off, or flip D3 to A (R02);
- Bahrain PDPA prior notification and adequacy-list check for Singapore (R07);
- Saudi overseas controller registration (opened October 2026) and a documented legitimate-interest assessment (R08);
- the UAE has no legitimate-interest basis, and Oman reportedly needs express consent for transfers out of Oman (R09);
- health data from supplement and OTC comparisons (R10);
- minors aged 13-17 (R11).

Secondary items (R12-R24): the onboarding "never share" copy, the business-transfer clause, the anon-readable SECURITY DEFINER views, the retention periods, the n=5 survey cells in the repo, Sign in with Apple token revocation, no Supabase backups, the controller identity and home address, scraping risk, the complaint authorities, the device-ID derivation, and the manifest vs label mismatch.

UNVERIFIED
- whether the repo is still public and whether keys were rotated;
- Bahrain article numbers and whether Singapore is on the adequacy list (Resolution 42/2022);
- Saudi registration triggers for overseas controllers;
- the UAE executive regulations and Art 4 detail;
- whether Oman, Qatar and Kuwait laws apply to a foreign app;
- the Meta policy quote (the page renders client-side);
- the Microsoft "does not sell" wording;
- the per-cell ASC role matrix;
- whether the DPAs have been executed;
- what consent the survey respondents gave.

Sources:
- Apple App Review Guidelines: https://developer.apple.com/app-store/review/guidelines/
- Apple App Privacy details: https://developer.apple.com/app-store/app-privacy-details/
- Apple Developer Program License Agreement, 3.3.3(B) and the HealthKit de-identification clause: https://developer.apple.com/support/terms/apple-developer-program-license-agreement/
- Apple account deletion guidance: https://developer.apple.com/support/offering-account-deletion-in-your-app/
- OpenAI data sharing help: https://help.openai.com/en/articles/10306912
- OpenAI privacy policy: https://openai.com/policies/row-privacy-policy/
- Google privacy policy: https://policies.google.com/privacy
- Microsoft privacy statement: https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/privacy/privacystatement
- Amazon privacy notice: https://www.amazon.com/gp/help/customer/display.html?nodeId=GX7NJQ4ZB8MHFRNJ
- UAE government data protection page: https://u.ae/en/about-the-uae/digital-uae/data/data-protection-laws
- Secondary only (marked UNVERIFIED where relied on): https://www.clydeco.com/en/insights/2022/04/bahrain-issues-new-privacy-guidelines and https://www.clydeco.com/en/insights/2026/10/sdaia-opens-controller-registration-for-overseas-o

Nothing was written in any repository. Scratch downloads are in the scratchpad: legalref/*.html(.txt), en.json, priors.json, asc_packet.md, strip.py.

